TASK

Return the figures that matter. Not every number in the file — the ones a consultant would point at.

What matters: volumes, prices, revenue, costs, capacity, utilisation, conversion, churn, headcount, hours, wait times, refunds, no-shows. Totals and averages usually matter more than individual rows. A number that moves over time matters more than a single snapshot — if the file shows twelve months, the figure worth returning is the total, the average, or the trend, not January.

What does not: row ids, order numbers, phone numbers, postcodes, dates on their own, and anything that is a label rather than a measure.

RULES

1. Every figure must come from ONE cell you name. Give the exact address. We check the cell against your value and throw away anything that does not match, so a guessed address loses a figure that was really there.

2. If the number you want is a total the file does not contain — a column you would have to add up yourself — do not report it. Report a cell that exists. An honest missing total is better than a sum we cannot check.

3. Say what the figure MEANS in the business's own terms, in a few words. "Monthly revenue" and "Sessions delivered in March" are useful. "Value in C4" is not — we already know that.

4. Give the unit and the time basis when the file makes them clear. Leave them empty when it does not. Do not infer that a column is monthly because it looks monthly.

5. Return at most 25 figures. An empty list is a real answer: some files are address books, and saying so is more useful than dressing one up as financial evidence.

Respond with ONLY this JSON shape, no markdown fences, no commentary:

{
  "figures": [
    {
      "table": "<the table's name only, without the word TABLE and without quotes>",
      "cell": "<e.g. C4>",
      "value": <the number, as a number and nothing else>,
      "unit": "<e.g. USD, sessions, hours, %>",
      "time_basis": "<day | week | month | year, or empty>",
      "means": "<what this figure is, in the business's terms>"
    }
  ]
}
